WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:02.720
What if just one
lifestyle change

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.520
could help you
avoid getting cancer,

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:07.360
or diabetes,
or heart disease,

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:08.800
or high blood pressure?

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.080
This one change could cut
your risk of chronic disease

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.920
and add years to your life.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:17.680
Well, the simple solution to
so many of these problems

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:20.000
is to eat a healthy diet.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.360
In other words, one centered
around whole plant foods.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.960
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.840
I'm your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:32.720
Today we look at some
of the latest research

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.720
on leaky gut syndrome.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.280
Did you know that
common drugs, foods,

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:41.040
and beverages can disrupt the
integrity of our intestinal barrier?

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:42.960
Here’s the story.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:47.520
Intestinal permeability,
the leakiness of our gut,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.320
may be a new target for
disease prevention and therapy.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:55.040
With all its little folds, our
intestinal barrier covers a surface

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.200
of more than 4,000 square feet —

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.360
that's bigger than a tennis
court — and requires

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.360
approximately 40% of our body's
total energy expenditure to maintain.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:07.840
Mounting evidence implicates the
disruption of intestinal barrier

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.720
integrity in the development
of numerous ailments

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.640
such as inflammatory bowel disease.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:17.200
Here, researchers measured intestinal
permeability using blue food coloring.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.960
It stays in your gut if you're healthy,
but can be detected in the blood

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.480
of extremely sick individuals as
their gut barrier breaks down.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.560
You don't have to end up in the ICU
to develop a leaky gut though.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:32.960
Simply taking some aspirin
or ibuprofen can do the trick.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.000
Indeed, taking two regular aspirin,
or two extra-strength aspirin just once

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.760
can increase the leakiness of
your gut. These results suggest

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:45.280
even healthy individuals should
be cautious with aspirin use

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.880
as it may result in
gastrointestinal barrier dysfunction.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.000
What about buffered aspirin?
It doesn't make any difference:

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.000
both regular aspirin and Bufferin
produced multiple erosions

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:00.320
in the inner lining of the
stomach and intestines.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.080
Put a scope down people's throats
and you can see extensive erosions

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:08.160
and redness inside 90% of people
taking aspirin or Bufferin

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.160
in their recommended doses.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.800
How many hours does it take
for the damage to occur?

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.000
None. It happens within 5 minutes.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.040
Acetaminophen, sold as Tylenol
in the U.S., would be a better choice,

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.360
unless you have problems
with your liver.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.400
And rather than making things better,
Vitamin C supplements appeared

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.160
to make the aspirin-induced increase
in gut leakiness even worse.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.800
Interestingly, this may be why NSAID
drugs like aspirin, ibuprofen,&nbsp;

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:37.840
and naproxen

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.920
are involved in up to 25% of
food-induced anaphylaxis;

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:45.680
in other words, increasing the odds
of life-threatening food allergy attacks

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.760
by more than 10-fold,
presumably because these drugs

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:52.240
increase the leakiness
of the intestinal barrier,

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.960
causing tiny food particles
to slip into the bloodstream.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:59.840
Okay, but why can exercise
increase risk, too?

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:05.200
Strenuous exercise, like an hour
at 70% of maximum capacity,

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.440
can divert so much blood to the muscles,
away from your internal organs

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.640
that it can cause transient
injury to your intestines,

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.720
causing mild gut leakiness.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.000
But this can be aggravated
if athletes take ibuprofen

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.920
or any of the other NSAID drugs,
which is unfortunately all too common.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.640
Alcohol can also be a risk factor
for food allergy attacks for

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.440
the same reason:
increasing gut leakiness.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.560
But cut out the alcohol
and your gut can heal up.

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.080
What other dietary components
can make a difference?

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:41.280
Elevated consumption of saturated fat,
which is found in meat, dairy, and junk,

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.200
can cause the growth of bad bacteria,
which make the rotten egg gas

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.280
hydrogen sulfide, which can
degrade the protective mucus layer.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.920
It is said to be clear that high fat diets,
in general, negatively impact

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:57.360
intestinal health by disrupting
the intestinal barrier system

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.200
through a variety of mechanisms.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.360
But most of the vast array of studies
cited on the negative effects

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.640
of high-fat diet on gut leakiness
were done on lab animals

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:07.920
or in a petri dish.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:12.160
You don't know for sure
until you put it to the test.

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:15.760
Rates of obesity and other
cardiometabolic disorders

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.520
have increased rapidly in parallel

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:21.520
with a transition from traditional
lower-fat diets to higher-fat diets.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:25.520
We know a disturbance in our good gut
flora has been shown to be associated

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.560
with a high risk of many
of these same diseases.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.680
And studies using rodents
suggest that a high-fat diet

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:36.240
unbalances the microbiome and impairs
the gut barrier, resulting in disease.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:40.880
To connect all the dots, though, we
need a human interventional trial,

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.840
and here we go: a 6-month
randomized controlled-feeding trial

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.280
on the effects of dietary
fat on gut microbiota.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.320
And indeed, higher fat consumption
appeared to be associated

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.632
with unfavorable changes
in the gut microbiome, and

00:04:55.632 --> 00:05:00.320
proinflammatory factors in the blood.
And note: this wasn't meat and dairy.

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:04.400
The researchers just swapped in
refined carbs in place of refined fats,

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.560
like white rice and white flour for oil.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:10.000
These findings suggest countries
westernizing their diets

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.800
should advise against increasing
intakes of dietary fat,

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.520
while countries that have already
adopted westernized diets

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.280
should consider cutting down.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.440
So far, we've discussed things
to prevent a leaky gut.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.840
What about foods to heal a leaky gut?

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.960
That's what we'll cover next.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.880
Our intestinal tract is the largest barrier
between us and the environment.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.600
More than what we touch or breathe,
what we eat is our largest exposure to

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:42.720
the outside world. Normally, our entire
gastro-intestinal tract is impervious

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.360
to what is inside of it, allowing
our body to pick and choose

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:46.480
what comes in or out.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:51.120
But there are things that may make
our gut leaky, and chief among them

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:55.280
is our diet. What happens is
the Standard American Diet

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:59.920
can cause gut dysbiosis, meaning
a disruption in our gut microbiome,

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:03.760
which can lead to intestinal inflammation
and a leaky intestinal barrier.

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:06.560
Then tiny bits of undigested
food, microbes, and toxins

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:10.320
can slip uninvited through our
gut lining into our bloodstream

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.960
and trigger chronic
systemic inflammation.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:16.880
To avoid this dysbiosis and
intestinal inflammation,

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:21.360
plants should be preferred.
Vegetarian diet gut bacteria

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:23.920
are associated with
intestinal microbiome balance,

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:28.720
high bacterial biodiversity, and
integrity of the intestinal barrier.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:32.160
They tend to suffer from
markedly fewer uremic toxins,

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:34.160
like indole and p-cresol,

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:38.240
and because fiber is the primary
food for our gut microbiome,

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:40.320
the gut bacteria of those
eating plant-based diets

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:42.960
produce more of the good stuff,
the short-chain fatty acids

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:46.480
that fulfill a protective and
nourishing role for the cells lining

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:50.240
our gut, ensuring the preservation
of the intestinal barrier.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:54.560
Plant fiber is of prime
importance to the preservation

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:56.480
of the intestinal barrier integrity,

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:02.080
but you can't know for sure
until you put it to the test.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:06.160
People were given whole grains,
beans and lentils, fruits, vegetables,

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:11.440
and nuts and seeds, and got a
significant reduction in zonulin levels.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:16.480
Zonulin is a protein responsible
for the disassembling

00:07:16.480 --> 00:07:20.160
of the tight junctions between gut
lining cells, and so it's a biomarker

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.040
that reflects an impairment
of the intestinal barrier.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:27.440
In other words, zonulin is considered
to be a useful marker of a leaky gut.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:31.040
But since adding all those plants
seemed to lower levels, that may

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:34.560
imply that appropriate fiber intake
helps to maintain the proper structure

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:36.480
and function of the intestinal barrier.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:40.480
But whole healthy plant foods
have a lot more than fiber.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:42.480
How do we know it's the fiber?

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:45.040
And the study didn't even
have a control group.

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:49.200
That's why they say gut permeability
might be improved by dietary fiber.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.680
To prove cause-and-effect, it'd be nice
to have a randomized double-blind

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:57.440
crossover study where you
compare the effect of the same food

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:00.880
with or without fiber,
and here we go.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:04.960
People were randomized to pasta,
with or without added fiber,

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:09.040
and there was a significant
drop in zonulin levels

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:10.240
in the added fiber group,

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:13.360
so fiber does appear
to improve gut leakiness.

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:16.240
Any plant foods in particular
that may help?

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:20.160
Curcumin, the yellow pigment
in the spice turmeric,

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:23.120
can help prevent the intestinal
damage done by ibuprofen-type

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:26.320
drugs, but that's in rats.

00:08:26.320 --> 00:08:29.440
Similar protection was noted for
the broccoli compound sulforaphane,

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:33.600
but that was in mice.
No human studies on broccoli yet,

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:36.640
but there was a study on three
days of the equivalent of about

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:41.440
2 to 3 teaspoons a day of turmeric,
which did reduce the gastrointestinal

00:08:41.440 --> 00:08:43.840
barrier damage caused by exercise.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:48.080
Less may work, too, but no smaller
doses have been put to the test.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:51.280
If you ask alternative
medicine practitioners

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:53.520
what treatments they
use for a leaky gut,

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:58.240
#1 on the list, after reducing
alcohol consumption, is zinc.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:03.920
Zinc doesn't just protect against
aspirin-like drug-induced damage in rats.

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:07.280
When put to the test in
a randomized trial of humans,

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:08.720
the same thing was found.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:13.360
The NSAID drug indomethacin caused
a three-fold rise in gut permeability

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:15.920
as one would expect
from that class of drugs,

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:20.560
but when they were also taking zinc,
this prevented the rise in permeability,

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:24.160
strongly suggesting a small
intestinal protective effect.

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:28.880
The dose they used was
massive though: 75 mg a day;

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:32.400
that's nearly twice the tolerable
upper daily limit for zinc.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:35.840
What about getting zinc
just at regular food doses?

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:40.400
There was a significant improvement
in gut leakiness even with a dose

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:42.960
of just three milligrams of zinc,

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:46.880
suggesting that even relatively low
zinc supplementation may work.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:49.200
You can get the three
extra milligrams of zinc

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.977
in your daily diet
eating a cup of cooked lentils.

